Description: avfilter: add agapfill, which fills gaps between audio frames with silence
 ffmpeg cuts the frames of an audio encoder with a fixed frame length by
 sample count. receive_samples() in fftools/sync_queue.c takes the next
 samples whatever their timestamps say, and audio_frame_queue in
 libavcodec stamps packets on the same assumption. When audio frames with
 a gap between them reach such an encoder, the first samples after the
 gap complete the frame before it and play early by the length of the
 gap. A resampler has the same fault through its delay line. No stock
 filter writes the gap as silence without also trimming audio whose
 timestamps overlap and holding the whole gap in memory.
 .
 agapfill fills every gap ahead of such consumers and never drops a
 sample. It compares the timestamp of each frame with the end of the
 previous frame. A forward step above the threshold, 2 ms by default, is
 a gap, and the filter writes silence up to the frame's own timestamp. A
 step at or below the threshold, forward or backward, passes through:
 the output takes the same step, as it does without the filter. The
 frame keeps its own timestamp, or stays as far from it as an earlier
 joined overlap left the output. A longer overlap or backward step
 writes no silence, and the frame follows the previous one. Each step
 is decided alone, so drift never adds up to a gap. The start option
 puts silence from a given time up to the first frame.
 .
 A short step passes through instead of joining the frame to the one
 before it. Joined, each step moved the audio after it, so audio whose
 timestamps run 0.02 % fast played 120 ms from its timestamps after
 600 s, and a seek within the encode kept that error. An encode without
 the filter keeps each step, and so does the filter.
 .
 The filter writes silence one frame at a time, when the next filter
 asks for a frame, so memory does not grow with the gap. max_gap makes a
 longer gap an error.
 .
 The filter counts timestamps in samples. A timestamp that lies beyond
 the 64-bit range once counted so, for example 10^18 ms at 48 kHz,
 stops at the largest or the smallest timestamp, and so does start.
 av_rescale_q_rnd() answers INT64_MIN there, the value that means no
 timestamp. Read so, the frame would follow the previous one, and max_gap
 would let the gap or the lead before it pass.
 .
 Like apad, the filter outputs frames without new input while it writes
 a gap. ffmpeg's read_frames() returns to the scheduler after each step
 only for a graph that it counts as having a source, and only the
 scheduler can end a graph whose outputs are all finished. A graph with
 agapfill was not counted. Once the video of such a graph had ended, an
 audio output finished by -frames:a kept read_frames() writing the rest
 of the gap for nothing, and a gap of 31,000 years never ended. ffmpeg
 now counts agapfill as it counts apad.
 .
 streamarr/tests/agapfill.py checks the filter.
