--- FFmpeg.orig/doc/filters.texi
+++ FFmpeg/doc/filters.texi
@@ -2073,6 +2073,106 @@ Set softness applied inside thresholding
 
 This filter supports the all above options as @ref{commands}.
 
+@section agapfill
+
+Fill gaps between audio frames with silence, so that the output has no gap
+left.
+
+Some encoders and filters count samples and ignore timestamps. When two audio
+frames with a gap between them reach one of those, the samples after the gap
+follow the samples before it directly, and play early by the length of the
+gap. This filter writes the missing time as silence first.
+
+The filter never drops or trims a sample. It compares the timestamp of each
+frame with the end of the previous frame, which is that frame's own timestamp
+plus its length.
+
+@itemize
+@item
+A forward step longer than @option{threshold} is a gap. The filter writes
+silence up to the timestamp of the later frame, so that frame is output at its
+own time. When earlier overlaps have already carried the output past that
+timestamp, the filter writes no silence.
+
+@item
+A step at or below @option{threshold}, forward or backward, passes through.
+The output takes the same step, as it does without this filter. The later
+frame keeps its own timestamp, or stays as far from it as an earlier joined
+overlap left the output. That covers rounded timestamps and timestamps that
+drift against the sample count.
+
+@item
+A longer overlap or backward step writes no silence, and the later frame
+follows the previous frame directly. After such a step the output times are
+later than the input timestamps by the length of the step, until a gap puts a
+frame at its own time again.
+@end itemize
+
+Each step is decided alone. Steps at or below the threshold never add up to a
+gap.
+
+The silence of a gap reaches the later frame's own timestamp, so it is shorter
+by what earlier overlaps have left between the output and the timestamps. For
+example, at 48 kHz, an overlap of 2,400 samples leaves the output 2,400 samples
+after the timestamps. A gap of 384,000 samples after it gets 381,600 samples
+of silence.
+
+The output time base is one sample. Each output frame begins where the
+previous one ends, moved by any step that passes through. A frame without a
+timestamp follows the previous frame directly. A timestamp or a
+@option{start} that lies beyond the 64-bit range once counted in samples
+stops at the largest or the smallest timestamp, so a gap up to such a frame
+is filled, or fails on @option{max_gap}, like any other. The filter writes no
+silence after the last frame. Use @code{apad} for that.
+
+The filter writes silence one frame at a time, when the next filter asks for
+a frame. It holds at most one input frame and one frame of silence, however
+long the gap is.
+
+The filter accepts the following options:
+
+@table @option
+@item threshold
+Set the longest step between two frames that passes through. A longer forward
+step is a gap, and a longer backward step is joined. Default is 2
+milliseconds. When the input time base is not one sample, the value must be
+at least one tick of that time base plus one sample. Timestamps rounded to
+that time base differ by that much where the audio has no gap.
+
+@item start
+Set the time at which the output begins when the first frame is later. The
+filter writes silence from this time up to the first frame, however short
+that stretch is. A time between two samples counts as the later sample. When
+the first frame is at or before this time, the filter writes no leading
+silence and outputs the frame whole. By default the output begins at the first
+frame.
+
+@item max_gap
+Set the longest run of silence the filter may write. The filter fails when a
+gap or the leading silence is longer. Default is 0, which sets no limit.
+
+@item packet_size
+Set the number of samples in each frame of silence. Default is 4096.
+@end table
+
+@subsection Examples
+
+@itemize
+@item
+Fill every gap in the audio ahead of an encoder that takes frames of a fixed
+length:
+@example
+ffmpeg -i input.mkv -af agapfill -c:a flac output.flac
+@end example
+
+@item
+Also write silence from zero up to audio that starts late, and fail on a gap
+of more than ten minutes:
+@example
+agapfill=start=0:max_gap=600
+@end example
+@end itemize
+
